# Line-ending policy.
#
# The IFC substrate manifest is BYTE-HASHED for cross-host drift detection
# (stingtools_core/substrate.py + the server's SubstrateManifestProvider).
# If a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true) materialised these files with CRLF
# while a Linux CI/Docker server saw LF, the two halves would hash differently
# for byte-identical content and drift forever. We pin the substrate to LF so the
# on-disk bytes are canonical on every OS — the root-cause fix. (The hashers also
# LF-normalise as defence in depth; this keeps any *other* byte-hash consumer safe
# too.)
#
# Scoped deliberately to the substrate to avoid a repo-wide renormalisation diff.

shared/ifc/**        text eol=lf
**/_manifest.json    text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the smoke-test source and its generated markdown.
# tools/check_smoke_test.py asserts the committed REVIT_SMOKE_TEST.md is a
# byte-identical regeneration of smoke_test.json, and the CI gate re-runs the
# generator and calls `git diff --exit-code`. tools/build_smoke_test.py writes
# LF; without this pin a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true materialises
# CRLF and the diff is non-empty on a tree nobody edited. (The checker itself
# LF-normalises before comparing, as defence in depth — this stops the *git*
# half of the check crying wolf.)
docs/examples/*/smoke_test.json       text eol=lf
docs/examples/*/REVIT_SMOKE_TEST.md   text eol=lf
docs/examples/*/*.docx                binary

# Same reasoning again for the three files tools/param_binding_resolver.py writes.
# binding-spec-drift.yml regenerates them on Linux and calls `git diff` — so if a
# Windows checkout materialised CRLF, every PR touching a parameter would fail the
# gate on line endings alone and the real diff would be invisible underneath.
# The resolver now pins its own writers to LF (see the LF constant at its top);
# this stops git undoing that on checkout.
StingTools/Data/RESOLVED_BINDINGS.csv text eol=lf
docs/RESOLVED_BINDINGS.csv            text eol=lf
docs/binding_gaps.csv                 text eol=lf

# PARAM-1: the same reasoning again for the parameter files. param-csv-drift.yml
# regenerates MR_PARAMETERS.csv on Linux and calls `git diff` -- so if a Windows
# checkout materialised CRLF, every PR touching a parameter would fail the gate on
# line endings alone with the real diff invisible underneath. The generator now pins
# its writer to LF (see the io.open(newline) call at the end of sync_csv_from_txt.py);
# this stops git undoing that on checkout. The .txt is pinned too: it is the
# generator's INPUT, and a CRLF input would change what the generator reads.
StingTools/Data/MR_PARAMETERS.csv     text eol=lf
StingTools/Data/MR_PARAMETERS.txt     text eol=lf
